  <!-- PART 1: ACTIVITY NO. 3 -->
  <!-- ========================================================================= -->
  <div style="background-color: #1e3a2f; color: #ffffff; padding: 6pt 10pt; margin-top: 10pt; border-radius: 4pt;">
    <h1 style="color: #ffffff; border: none; margin: 0; font-size: 16pt;">
      PART I: ACTIVITY NO. 3 — CAMPUS PLASTIC CONSUMPTION AUDIT &amp; ACTION SIMULATOR
    </h1>
    <p style="color: #a7f3d0; margin: 2pt 0 0 0; font-size: 10pt;">
      Course Mandate: "Track your plastic consumption for a week and develop practical alternatives to single-use plastics"
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>1.1 Project Overview &amp; Research Problem</h2>
  <p>
    Single-use plastics (SUPs) dominate Indian higher education campuses due to micro-pricing, rapid retail transactions, and convenience-driven student habits. Canteen meals, packaged hydration bottles, takeaway parcels, and hostel personal care sachets create vast volumes of unsegregated, contaminated plastic waste. In alignment with India's <em>Plastic Waste Management Rules (2022 Amendment)</em> and the nationwide ban on select single-use plastics, our student investigation team conducted an empirical 7-day characterization audit followed by a structured behavioral intervention challenge across our campus.
  </p>

  <h2>1.2 Scientific Methodology &amp; Quality Control</h2>
  <p>
    To eliminate reporting bias and self-censorship, our team implemented a three-tier tracking methodology:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Daily Physical Separation &amp; Collection Box:</strong> Dedicated dry segregation bins were maintained in hostel quarters and day bags. All empty packaging generated between morning lectures, dining halls, and evening study sessions was retained rather than discarded in campus trash cans.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Analytical Scale Mass Measurement &amp; Polymer Resin Verification:</strong> Standard weights were determined using a 0.1g laboratory electronic precision balance. Resin identification codes (RIC) were documented:
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Resin Code #1 (PET):</strong> 500ml/1L mineral water bottles = <strong>18.0 g/unit</strong> (100% recyclable if unsoiled).</li>
        <li><strong>Resin Code #7 (Multilayer Plastic Film / BOPP):</strong> Fused aluminum-plastic metallized snack pouches (Lays, Kurkure) = <strong>4.0 g/unit</strong> (Non-recyclable).</li>
        <li><strong>Resin Code #7 (Flexible Metallized Film):</strong> Chocolate wrappers (Dairy Milk, KitKat) = <strong>2.5 g/unit</strong> (Non-recyclable).</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>UPI Payment Receipt Cross-Auditing:</strong> Every logged plastic item was cross-referenced against daily Unified Payments Interface (UPI) transaction records (Google Pay / Paytm) at campus vendors to ensure zero missed transactions.
    </li>
  </ol>

