  <h2>1.7 Campus Policy Recommendations</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Certified RO Hydration Coolers:</strong> Affix weekly microbiological and TDS laboratory test certificates beside all campus RO water coolers to eliminate student hesitation regarding drinking water quality, directly curbing packaged bottle sales.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Canteen BYOB &amp; Dabba Incentives:</strong> Contractually mandate campus food vendors to offer a ₹1 discount for students bringing personal steel mugs for chai and a ₹5 discount for takeaway food in personal steel tiffins.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Hostel Bulk Dispensary Program:</strong> Introduce hostel welfare committee procurement of bulk 5L refill cans for shampoo, handwash, and detergent at hostel reception counters, eliminating high-volume multi-layer plastic sachet generation.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <div class="page-break"></div>

  <!-- ========================================================================= -->
  <!-- PART 2: ACTIVITY NO. 4 -->
  <!-- ========================================================================= -->
  <div style="background-color: #0f766e; color: #ffffff; padding: 6pt 10pt; margin-top: 10pt; border-radius: 4pt;">
    <h1 style="color: #ffffff; border: none; margin: 0; font-size: 16pt;">
      PART II: ACTIVITY NO. 4 — ECOFLOW: NATURE-BASED WASTEWATER TREATMENT SIMULATOR
    </h1>
    <p style="color: #ccfbf1; margin: 2pt 0 0 0; font-size: 10pt;">
      Course Mandate: "Build a nature-based solution for wastewater treatment"
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>2.1 Engineering Context &amp; Wastewater Characterization</h2>
  <p>
    Conventional mechanized wastewater treatment plants (e.g., Moving Bed Biofilm Reactors, MBBR, or Activated Sludge Plants, ASP) suffer from frequent motor breakdowns, high grid power consumption (0.8–1.2 kWh/m³), and expensive chemical flocculants. In contrast, <strong>Decentralized Wastewater Treatment Systems (DEWATS)</strong> leveraging <strong>Constructed Wetlands (CWs)</strong> utilize natural solar energy, physical gravity sedimentation, microbial biofilm assimilation, and botanical phytoremediation to purify water with zero chemical footprint.
  </p>

  <h3>Campus Influent Stream Characteristics</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Campus Effluent Origin</th>
        <th style="text-align: center;">Per Capita Flow (L/day)</th>
        <th style="text-align: right;">BOD (mg/L)</th>
        <th style="text-align: right;">TSS (mg/L)</th>
        <th style="text-align: right;">Turbidity (NTU)</th>
        <th style="text-align: right;">Oil &amp; Grease (mg/L)</th>
        <th>Key Contaminants Identified</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Hostel Wing Greywater</strong> (Showers &amp; Basins)</td>
        <td style="text-align: center;">45 L/student</td>
        <td style="text-align: right;">160</td>
        <td style="text-align: right;">140</td>
        <td style="text-align: right;">85</td>
        <td style="text-align: right;">24</td>
        <td>Surfactants (SLS), triclosan, skin sebum, lint</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Campus Canteen &amp; Mess</strong> (Kitchen sinks)</td>
        <td style="text-align: center;">25 L/meal</td>
        <td style="text-align: right;">480</td>
        <td style="text-align: right;">380</td>
        <td style="text-align: right;">260</td>
        <td style="text-align: right;">110</td>
        <td>Triglycerides, vegetable oils, starch, food scraps</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Combined Campus Block</strong> (Composite)</td>
        <td style="text-align: center;">40 L/capita</td>
        <td style="text-align: right;">290</td>
        <td style="text-align: right;">230</td>
        <td style="text-align: right;">160</td>
        <td style="text-align: right;">55</td>
        <td>Blended personal care greywater &amp; pantry wash</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>2.2 Hydraulic Design &amp; Spatial Sizing Formulation</h2>
  <p>
    The horizontal subsurface flow constructed wetland (HSSF-CW) was hydraulically sized according to standard environmental civil engineering design principles (Kadlec &amp; Wallace model):
  </p>

  <div class="callout-box">
    <strong>Governing Wetland Surface Area Sizing Formula:</strong><br/>
    <p style="font-family: monospace; font-size: 13pt; font-weight: bold; text-align: center; margin: 4pt 0;">
      A_s = (Q &times; HRT) / (d &times; &epsilon;)
    </p>
    <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 9.5pt;">
      Where:
      <strong>Q</strong> = Design daily flow volume (4,500 L/day = 4.5 m³/day for 100 students @ 45 L/day);
      <strong>HRT</strong> = Hydraulic Retention Time = 48 hours (2.0 days);
      <strong>d</strong> = Effective gravel bed depth = 0.60 meters;
      <strong>&epsilon;</strong> = Void ratio / porosity of washed crushed basalt gravel = 0.38 (38%).
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3>Calculated Spatial Dimensions for 100-Student Hostel Wing:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Required Wetland Surface Area (A_s):</strong> (4.5 &times; 2.0) / (0.60 &times; 0.38) = <strong>39.5 m²</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Aspect Ratio (Length : Width):</strong> Standard civil ratio of 2.5 : 1 (L = 2.5 &times; W).</li>
    <li><strong>Constructed Wetland Width (W):</strong> &radic;(39.5 / 2.5) = <strong>4.0 meters</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Constructed Wetland Length (L):</strong> 4.0 &times; 2.5 = <strong>10.0 meters</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Bed Slope:</strong> 1.0% bottom hydraulic slope for uniform gravity drainage.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2.3 The 4-Stage Nature-Based Treatment Train</h2>
  <p>
    The EcoFlow system employs a contiguous four-stage sequential train combining physical sedimentation, adsorption filtration, biological phytoremediation, and solar disinfection:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 12%;">Stage</th>
        <th style="width: 25%;">Unit Operation</th>
        <th style="width: 15%;">Design HRT</th>
        <th style="width: 28%;">Internal Media Strata &amp; Depths</th>
        <th style="width: 20%;">Scientific Mechanism</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Stage 1</strong></td>
        <td><strong>Grit Chamber &amp; Baffled Grease Trap</strong></td>
        <td>2.5 Hours</td>
        <td>
          • Surface baffle skimmer (25cm clearance)<br/>
          • Dual settling basin (75cm depth)<br/>
          • Inverted pyramidal grit hopper (20cm)
        </td>
        <td>Stokes' Law gravity settling of dense food particles; density separation of floating free oils (&lt;0.92 SG).</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Stage 2</strong></td>
        <td><strong>Multi-Layer Bio-Sand &amp; Charcoal Filter</strong></td>
        <td>6.0 Hours</td>
        <td>
          • Fine silica sand 0.2–0.5mm (40 cm)<br/>
          • Activated hardwood charcoal (25 cm)<br/>
          • Coarse quartz sand 1–2mm (20 cm)<br/>
          • Graded river gravel underdrain (25 cm)
        </td>
        <td>Deep-bed straining of lint and colloidal TSS; surface biofilm assimilation; high internal surface area adsorption (&gt;350 m²/g) of surfactants and odors.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Stage 3</strong></td>
        <td><strong>Horizontal Subsurface Constructed Wetland (HSSF-CW)</strong></td>
        <td>48.0 Hours<br/>(2 Days)</td>
        <td>
          • Emergent macrophyte root zone (35 cm)<br/>
          • Washed basalt gravel 10–20mm (45 cm)<br/>
          • 1.2mm HDPE impermeable geomembrane liner
        </td>
        <td>Rhizospheric oxygen leakage creates aerobic/anoxic micro-zones; microbial nitrification-denitrification; botanical macronutrient uptake of N and P; sub-surface flow prevents mosquito breeding.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Stage 4</strong></td>
        <td><strong>Solar UV Polishing &amp; Storage Sump</strong></td>
        <td>12.0 Hours</td>
        <td>
          • Shallow Solar UV-A polishing weir (10 cm)<br/>
          • Treated clear water storage sump (1.5 m)<br/>
          • Submersible reuse pump
        </td>
        <td>Natural sunlight photo-oxidation; UV-A germicidal pathogen inactivation; aeration over weir cascade; clean buffer storage.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>2.4 Phytoremediation Botanical Matrix &amp; Performance Efficiency</h2>
  <p>
    The biological heart of the system relies on native Indian wetland macrophytes whose root networks host rich aerobic bacterial biofilms:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Canna Lily (<em>Canna indica</em>):</strong> Robust rhizome system with high tolerance for heavy surfactant loads and detergent phosphates. Delivers <strong>92% BOD and 90% phosphate uptake</strong> while adding aesthetic flowering landscapes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Vetiver Grass / Khus (<em>Chrysopogon zizanioides</em>):</strong> Flagship phytoremediator with massive, vertical fibrous root systems penetrating up to 1.5m deep into the gravel bed. Extensive aerenchyma tissue channels atmospheric oxygen down into the gravel matrix, driving <strong>95% BOD and 94% TSS reduction</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cattails (<em>Typha latifolia</em>):</strong> Extensive horizontal rhizomes and tubular stems with high capacity for nitrate removal (91%) and rhizosphere pathogen suppression.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Mixed Polyculture (Recommended):</strong> Co-planting Canna, Vetiver, and Typha creates ecological biodiversity, prevents seasonal die-off, and provides peak year-round pollutant attenuation.
    </li>
  </ul>

