  <h2>Part III: Integrated Campus Circular Sustainability Synthesis</h2>
  <p>
    Activities 3 and 4 converge to demonstrate that campus environmental sustainability cannot rely on fragmented, post-consumption cleanups. Instead, sustainable campuses must operate as <strong>closed-loop circular systems</strong>:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Campus Life Cycle Dimension</th>
        <th>Conventional Linear Approach</th>
        <th>Our Proposed Integrated Circular Approach</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Drinking Water Hydration</strong></td>
        <td>Single-use 1L PET bottles sold at food courts; discarded into municipal dumps.</td>
        <td>Certified, transparent campus RO cooler stations + student Daily Carry Kit (steel bottles).</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Campus Canteen Dining</strong></td>
        <td>Polypropylene chai cups and plastic meal takeout dabbas.</td>
        <td>BYOB mug discounts (₹1/cup) and stainless steel reusable tiffin box swaps.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Hostel Bath &amp; Laundry Effluent</strong></td>
        <td>Greywater discharged directly into storm drains, contaminating regional aquifers.</td>
        <td>Decentralized constructed wetland (EcoFlow) purifies water with native phytoremediation plants.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Landscape Irrigation</strong></td>
        <td>Over-extraction of deep groundwater borewells or expensive commercial water tankers.</td>
        <td>100% campus lawn and flowerbed irrigation served by treated wetland effluent.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

